    },
    "authorAssociation": "NONE",
    "body": "While looking at [this config](https://github.com/alex35mil/dotfiles/blob/master/home/.config/nvim/lua/plugins/alpha.lua#L37), it seems like `alpha-nvim` allows rendering the header section using a terminal command.\r\n\r\nI was wondering if such functionality is achievable in `mini.starter`\r\n\r\nSorry if the \"Ideas\" section is not the proper place for this, but since I couldn't find it in the docs, I thought it could be more of an idea rather than a \"general\" discussion",
    "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">While looking at <a href=\"https://github.com/alex35mil/dotfiles/blob/master/home/.config/nvim/lua/plugins/alpha.lua#L37\">this config</a>, it seems like <code class=\"notranslate\">alpha-nvim</code> allows rendering the header section using a terminal command.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I was wondering if such functionality is achievable in <code class=\"notranslate\">mini.starter</code></p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Sorry if the \"Ideas\" section is not the proper place for this, but since I couldn't find it in the docs, I thought it could be more of an idea rather than a \"general\" discussion</p>",
    "bodyText": "While looking at this config, it seems like alpha-nvim allows rendering the header section using a terminal command.\nI was wondering if such functionality is achievable in mini.starter\nSorry if the \"Ideas\" section is not the proper place for this, but since I couldn't find it in the docs, I thought it could be more of an idea rather than a \"general\" discussion",
    "category": {
        "createdAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z",
        "description": "Share ideas for new features",
        "emoji": ":bulb:",
        "emojiHTML": "<div>💡</div>",
        "id": "DIC_kwDOFoksYM4CAgqO",
        "isAnswerable": false,
        "name": "Ideas",
        "slug": "ideas",
        "updatedAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z"
    },
    "closed": true,
    "closedAt": "2023-10-03T17:52:26Z",
    "comment_count": 1,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                "login": "echasnovski",
                "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Sorry if the \"Ideas\" section is not the proper place for this, but since I couldn't find it in the docs, I thought it could be more of an idea rather than a \"general\" discussion\r\n\r\nNo worries, it's good.\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nBoth header and footer in config [can be callables](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/25c4bf4efac51aad5393e37949e68ac3e672a73c/doc/mini-starter.txt#L224-L232). Meaning that they can evaluate script and return its output with something like this:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal header = function() return vim.fn.system('~/.config/nvim/logo.sh') end\r\nrequire('mini.starter').setup({ header = header })\r\n```\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, this only works with the first variation (without colors), as escaped sequences are not supported.",
            "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Sorry if the \"Ideas\" section is not the proper place for this, but since I couldn't find it in the docs, I thought it could be more of an idea rather than a \"general\" discussion</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">No worries, it's good.</p>\n<hr>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Both header and footer in config <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/25c4bf4efac51aad5393e37949e68ac3e672a73c/doc/mini-starter.txt#L224-L232\">can be callables</a>. Meaning that they can evaluate script and return its output with something like this:</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"local header = function() return vim.fn.system('~/.config/nvim/logo.sh') end\nrequire('mini.starter').setup({ header = header })\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-k\">local</span> <span class=\"pl-en\">header</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">function</span>() <span class=\"pl-k\">return</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">fn</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">system</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>~/.config/nvim/logo.sh<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>) <span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>\n<span class=\"pl-c1\">require</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>mini.starter<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>).<span class=\"pl-c1\">setup</span>({ <span class=\"pl-smi\">header</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">header</span> })</pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Unfortunately, this only works with the first variation (without colors), as escaped sequences are not supported.</p>",
            "bodyText": "Sorry if the \"Ideas\" section is not the proper place for this, but since I couldn't find it in the docs, I thought it could be more of an idea rather than a \"general\" discussion\n\nNo worries, it's good.\n\nBoth header and footer in config can be callables. Meaning that they can evaluate script and return its output with something like this:\nlocal header = function() return vim.fn.system('~/.config/nvim/logo.sh') end\nrequire('mini.starter').setup({ header = header })\nUnfortunately, this only works with the first variation (without colors), as escaped sequences are not supported.",
